    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d489">

                <pb facs="0687" n="673" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg673"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/1–1049</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Major General Patrick J. Hurley</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Yenan</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1944-11-08">8 November, 1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Theodore White, correspondent for <hi rendition="#italic">Time</hi> and <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Life</hi>, told me that he had just talked to Chairman
                    Mao and Mao had told him that there <pb facs="0688" n="674" xml:id="pg674"/> was
                    not any possible chance of an agreement between him and Chiang Kai-shek. White
                    told me many of the reasons why Mao should not agree with the National
                    Government. White’s whole conversation was definitely against the mission with
                    which I am charged.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
